10 only connects with G9 to form a line of 2, which is weaker than the line of 3 formed by K10.\n3.  **Move F10**: This move creates a "Dead 4" (G9-H8-I7-F10) threatening to win on the next turn. However, it forces White to block at E11. After the block, Black has no follow-up, and White retains the initiative to play K10 and attack on row 10. Thus, it is strategically inferior to blocking the opponent\'s main threat.\n4.  **Move I6**: Creates a "Dead 4" vertically. Forces White to block at I5. This exhausts the potential of the vertical line without gaining a winning advantage or blocking White\'s row 10 threat.\n\nReason:\nBlack must prioritize defense against White\'s potential "Live 3" on row 10 (I10-J10). Playing at K10 neutralizes this threat completely. Furthermore, K10 is the most active defensive move because it simultaneously creates a diagonal 3-in-a-row for Black (I8-J9-K10), keeping the initiative and forcing the opponent to respond to Black\'s development.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 10}'}
